    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nI would use Dependency injection when the problem matches the behavior it provides: Dependency injection provides an object with its collaborators instead of requiring it to construct them internally. A concrete application is A Spring service can receive a repository through its constructor, making the dependency explicit and allowing a test to supply a stub or mock.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you can translate the concept into an engineering decision rather than reciting its definition.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the requirement.\n2. Check that Dependency injection provides the required behavior: Dependency injection provides an object with its collaborators instead of requiring it to construct them internally.\n3. Apply it in a concrete case: A Spring service can receive a repository through its constructor, making the dependency explicit and allowing a test to supply a stub or mock.\n4. Reassess the choice if the workload or constraints change: For Dependency injection, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the requirement, then explain why Dependency injection fits it. I would give the concrete example and mention the main limitation so the answer shows judgment, not just tool familiarity.\n\n**Example :**\nA Spring service can receive a repository through its constructor, making the dependency explicit and allowing a test to supply a stub or mock.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA production codebase maintained by an early-career engineer may use Dependency injection when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a Spring service can receive a repository through its constructor, making the dependency explicit and allowing a test to supply a stub or mock. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Dependency injection as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Dependency injection.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nDescribe the requirement that led you to choose it. “I would use it because it is popular” is much weaker than explaining the actual problem.",
