    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Dependency injection misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Dependency injection. Dependency injection provides an object with its collaborators instead of requiring it to construct them internally.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Dependency injection: Dependency injection provides an object with its collaborators instead of requiring it to construct them internally.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: A Spring service can receive a repository through its constructor, making the dependency explicit and allowing a test to supply a stub or mock.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: For Dependency injection, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nA Spring service can receive a repository through its constructor, making the dependency explicit and allowing a test to supply a stub or mock.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA production codebase maintained by an early-career engineer may use Dependency injection when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a Spring service can receive a repository through its constructor, making the dependency explicit and allowing a test to supply a stub or mock. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Dependency injection as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Dependency injection.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
