    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “What would you change about Dependency injection when the system grows or an important assumption stops being true?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to Dependency injection.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: Dependency injection provides an object with its collaborators instead of requiring it to construct them internally.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: A Spring service can receive a repository through its constructor, making the dependency explicit and allowing a test to supply a stub or mock.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: For Dependency injection, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nA Spring service can receive a repository through its constructor, making the dependency explicit and allowing a test to supply a stub or mock.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA production codebase maintained by an early-career engineer may use Dependency injection when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a Spring service can receive a repository through its constructor, making the dependency explicit and allowing a test to supply a stub or mock. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Dependency injection as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Dependency injection.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
