    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Logging misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Logging. Application logging records useful diagnostic events with enough context to understand behavior without exposing sensitive data.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Logging: Application logging records useful diagnostic events with enough context to understand behavior without exposing sensitive data.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: When an order fails, structured logs can include an order ID and operation name so the request can be traced without logging secrets or full payment details.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: For Logging, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nWhen an order fails, structured logs can include an order ID and operation name so the request can be traced without logging secrets or full payment details.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA production codebase maintained by an early-career engineer may use Logging when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, when an order fails, structured logs can include an order ID and operation name so the request can be traced without logging secrets or full payment details. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Logging as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Logging.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
