    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “What would you change about Logging when the system grows or an important assumption stops being true?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to Logging.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: Application logging records useful diagnostic events with enough context to understand behavior without exposing sensitive data.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: When an order fails, structured logs can include an order ID and operation name so the request can be traced without logging secrets or full payment details.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: For Logging, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nWhen an order fails, structured logs can include an order ID and operation name so the request can be traced without logging secrets or full payment details.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA production codebase maintained by an early-career engineer may use Logging when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, when an order fails, structured logs can include an order ID and operation name so the request can be traced without logging secrets or full payment details. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Logging as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Logging.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
