    shortDescription: "Frequently asked Software engineer 1-2 Years of expirance interview question about Unit testing.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nUnit tests validate small pieces of behavior quickly and deterministically.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Unit testing accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Unit tests validate small pieces of behavior quickly and deterministically.\n2. Use a concrete case: A discount-calculation function can be tested with normal prices, boundary values, and invalid inputs without starting the whole application.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Broader coverage can improve confidence, but execution time, flakiness, and maintenance cost grow if checks are added without regard to risk.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Unit testing first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA discount-calculation function can be tested with normal prices, boundary values, and invalid inputs without starting the whole application.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA production codebase maintained by an early-career engineer may use Unit testing when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a discount-calculation function can be tested with normal prices, boundary values, and invalid inputs without starting the whole application. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Unit testing, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
