    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe main value of Unit testing comes from the behavior it provides: Unit tests validate small pieces of behavior quickly and deterministically. It can be useful when that behavior reduces a specific engineering or business problem, as in this example: A discount-calculation function can be tested with normal prices, boundary values, and invalid inputs without starting the whole application.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can distinguish a genuine benefit from a feature list and understand what the benefit costs.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the behavior being provided: Unit tests validate small pieces of behavior quickly and deterministically.\n2. Connect it to the benefit shown by the example: A discount-calculation function can be tested with normal prices, boundary values, and invalid inputs without starting the whole application.\n3. State the trade-off: Broader coverage can improve confidence, but execution time, flakiness, and maintenance cost grow if checks are added without regard to risk.\n4. Avoid claiming a benefit that is not supported by the actual workload.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name the practical benefits first, tie each one to the behavior, and then give the main trade-off. That shows I understand why the feature is valuable rather than simply listing advantages.\n\n**Example :**\nA discount-calculation function can be tested with normal prices, boundary values, and invalid inputs without starting the whole application.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA production codebase maintained by an early-career engineer may use Unit testing when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a discount-calculation function can be tested with normal prices, boundary values, and invalid inputs without starting the whole application. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain why the benefit matters for the workload. A benefit without a condition is usually too broad for a strong engineering answer.",
