    shortDescription: "Frequently asked Software engineer 1-2 Years of expirance interview question about Unit testing.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Unit testing misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Unit testing. Unit tests validate small pieces of behavior quickly and deterministically.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Unit testing: Unit tests validate small pieces of behavior quickly and deterministically.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: A discount-calculation function can be tested with normal prices, boundary values, and invalid inputs without starting the whole application.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: Broader coverage can improve confidence, but execution time, flakiness, and maintenance cost grow if checks are added without regard to risk.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nA discount-calculation function can be tested with normal prices, boundary values, and invalid inputs without starting the whole application.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA production codebase maintained by an early-career engineer may use Unit testing when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a discount-calculation function can be tested with normal prices, boundary values, and invalid inputs without starting the whole application. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Unit testing misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Unit testing. Unit tests validate small pieces of behavior quickly and deterministically.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Unit testing: Unit tests validate small pieces of behavior quickly and deterministically.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: A discount-calculation function can be tested with normal prices, boundary values, and invalid inputs without starting the whole application.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: Broader coverage can improve confidence, but execution time, flakiness, and maintenance cost grow if checks are added without regard to risk.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nA discount-calculation function can be tested with normal prices, boundary values, and invalid inputs without starting the whole application.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA production codebase maintained by an early-career engineer may use Unit testing when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a discount-calculation function can be tested with normal prices, boundary values, and invalid inputs without starting the whole application. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
