    shortDescription: "Frequently asked Software engineer 1-2 Years of expirance interview question about Integration testing.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nIntegration testing is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. Broader coverage can improve confidence, but execution time, flakiness, and maintenance cost grow if checks are added without regard to risk. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what Integration testing gives you: Integration tests verify interactions among components such as an application and database or external service boundary.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: Broader coverage can improve confidence, but execution time, flakiness, and maintenance cost grow if checks are added without regard to risk.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: An API integration test can start the service with a test database, call the endpoint, and verify the persisted result and returned status together.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nAn API integration test can start the service with a test database, call the endpoint, and verify the persisted result and returned status together.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA production codebase maintained by an early-career engineer may use Integration testing when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, an API integration test can start the service with a test database, call the endpoint, and verify the persisted result and returned status together. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
