    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nSQL tuning improves query execution by examining predicates, indexes, plans, data volume, and access patterns.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define SQL tuning accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: SQL tuning improves query execution by examining predicates, indexes, plans, data volume, and access patterns.\n2. Use a concrete case: If a query is slow, inspect its execution plan, row estimates, indexes, joins, and returned data volume before changing the SQL blindly.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Faster reads can come with extra storage, write overhead, or plan complexity, so the access pattern should justify the change.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define SQL tuning first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nIf a query is slow, inspect its execution plan, row estimates, indexes, joins, and returned data volume before changing the SQL blindly.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA production codebase maintained by an early-career engineer may use SQL tuning when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, if a query is slow, inspect its execution plan, row estimates, indexes, joins, and returned data volume before changing the SQL blindly. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Relying on intuition instead of the actual query plan.\n- Ignoring row counts and data distribution.\n- Fixing one query while creating a write, memory, or locking problem elsewhere.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor SQL tuning, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
