    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen SQL tuning misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to SQL tuning. SQL tuning improves query execution by examining predicates, indexes, plans, data volume, and access patterns.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls SQL tuning: SQL tuning improves query execution by examining predicates, indexes, plans, data volume, and access patterns.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: If a query is slow, inspect its execution plan, row estimates, indexes, joins, and returned data volume before changing the SQL blindly.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: Faster reads can come with extra storage, write overhead, or plan complexity, so the access pattern should justify the change.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nIf a query is slow, inspect its execution plan, row estimates, indexes, joins, and returned data volume before changing the SQL blindly.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA production codebase maintained by an early-career engineer may use SQL tuning when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, if a query is slow, inspect its execution plan, row estimates, indexes, joins, and returned data volume before changing the SQL blindly. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Relying on intuition instead of the actual query plan.\n- Ignoring row counts and data distribution.\n- Fixing one query while creating a write, memory, or locking problem elsewhere.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
