    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nAuthentication is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. Stronger controls reduce risk but can add latency, operational work, or user friction; the control should match the threat and resource being protected. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what Authentication gives you: Authentication verifies identity before protected operations are performed.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: Stronger controls reduce risk but can add latency, operational work, or user friction; the control should match the threat and resource being protected.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: A service can authenticate a request using a validated session or token, then establish the identity that later authorization checks use.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nA service can authenticate a request using a validated session or token, then establish the identity that later authorization checks use.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA production codebase maintained by an early-career engineer may use Authentication when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a service can authenticate a request using a validated session or token, then establish the identity that later authorization checks use. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating internal location as proof of trust.\n- Logging secrets or sensitive data while trying to improve diagnosis.\n- Checking authentication but forgetting authorization at the resource boundary.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
