    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nIt matters because debugging combines reproduction, evidence gathering, hypothesis testing, and validation of the fix. In practice, the example shows how that behavior is used: if an API starts returning 500 errors, an engineer can reproduce the request, inspect the stack trace and logs, identify the failing layer, and verify the fix with a regression test.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe question is looking for reasoning rather than another definition. Explain the problem the concept solves, then say what would make you choose a different approach.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State the problem or need.\n2. Explain how Debugging addresses it: Debugging combines reproduction, evidence gathering, hypothesis testing, and validation of the fix.\n3. Tie that benefit to the concrete situation: If an API starts returning 500 errors, an engineer can reproduce the request, inspect the stack trace and logs, identify the failing layer, and verify the fix with a regression test.\n4. State the condition that can make the approach a poor fit: For Debugging, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nAnswer the “why” directly, then connect it to a real engineering constraint. I would give one concrete situation and close by saying what would make me choose another approach.\n\n**Example :**\nIf an API starts returning 500 errors, an engineer can reproduce the request, inspect the stack trace and logs, identify the failing layer, and verify the fix with a regression test.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA production codebase maintained by an early-career engineer may use Debugging when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, if an API starts returning 500 errors, an engineer can reproduce the request, inspect the stack trace and logs, identify the failing layer, and verify the fix with a regression test. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Debugging as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Debugging.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nDo not repeat the definition as your whole answer. Explain the problem, the practical value, and the condition that would change your decision.",
