    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nDebugging is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. For Debugging, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what Debugging gives you: Debugging combines reproduction, evidence gathering, hypothesis testing, and validation of the fix.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: For Debugging, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: If an API starts returning 500 errors, an engineer can reproduce the request, inspect the stack trace and logs, identify the failing layer, and verify the fix with a regression test.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nIf an API starts returning 500 errors, an engineer can reproduce the request, inspect the stack trace and logs, identify the failing layer, and verify the fix with a regression test.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA production codebase maintained by an early-career engineer may use Debugging when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, if an API starts returning 500 errors, an engineer can reproduce the request, inspect the stack trace and logs, identify the failing layer, and verify the fix with a regression test. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Debugging as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Debugging.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
