    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nTechnical debt is the future cost created by expedient design or implementation choices, including deferred cleanup and architectural shortcuts.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Technical debt accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Technical debt is the future cost created by expedient design or implementation choices, including deferred cleanup and architectural shortcuts.\n2. Use a concrete case: If a fragile module slows every release, the team can reserve capacity to simplify it rather than continuing to add workarounds around the same defect.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: For Technical debt, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Technical debt first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nIf a fragile module slows every release, the team can reserve capacity to simplify it rather than continuing to add workarounds around the same defect.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA production codebase maintained by an early-career engineer may use Technical debt when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, if a fragile module slows every release, the team can reserve capacity to simplify it rather than continuing to add workarounds around the same defect. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Technical debt as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Technical debt.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Technical debt, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
