    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nIt matters because technical debt is the future cost created by expedient design or implementation choices, including deferred cleanup and architectural shortcuts. In practice, the example shows how that behavior is used: if a fragile module slows every release, the team can reserve capacity to simplify it rather than continuing to add workarounds around the same defect.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe question is looking for reasoning rather than another definition. Explain the problem the concept solves, then say what would make you choose a different approach.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State the problem or need.\n2. Explain how Technical debt addresses it: Technical debt is the future cost created by expedient design or implementation choices, including deferred cleanup and architectural shortcuts.\n3. Tie that benefit to the concrete situation: If a fragile module slows every release, the team can reserve capacity to simplify it rather than continuing to add workarounds around the same defect.\n4. State the condition that can make the approach a poor fit: For Technical debt, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nAnswer the “why” directly, then connect it to a real engineering constraint. I would give one concrete situation and close by saying what would make me choose another approach.\n\n**Example :**\nIf a fragile module slows every release, the team can reserve capacity to simplify it rather than continuing to add workarounds around the same defect.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA production codebase maintained by an early-career engineer may use Technical debt when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, if a fragile module slows every release, the team can reserve capacity to simplify it rather than continuing to add workarounds around the same defect. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Technical debt as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Technical debt.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nDo not repeat the definition as your whole answer. Explain the problem, the practical value, and the condition that would change your decision.",
