    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen API compatibility misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to API compatibility. API compatibility means preserving the expected contract for existing consumers across changes, including request/response shape and relevant behavioral guarantees.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls API compatibility: API compatibility means preserving the expected contract for existing consumers across changes, including request/response shape and relevant behavioral guarantees.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: A service can add an optional response field without breaking clients, while changing the meaning or type of an existing required field may require a compatibility plan.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: For API compatibility, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nA service can add an optional response field without breaking clients, while changing the meaning or type of an existing required field may require a compatibility plan.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA growing production service may use API compatibility when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a service can add an optional response field without breaking clients, while changing the meaning or type of an existing required field may require a compatibility plan. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Confusing authentication with authorization.\n- Using inconsistent status/error semantics.\n- Changing an API contract without considering existing clients.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
