    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nQueues buffer work between producers and consumers so the producer does not have to perform all processing synchronously.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Queues accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Queues buffer work between producers and consumers so the producer does not have to perform all processing synchronously.\n2. Use a concrete case: A background worker can consume queued jobs in order without making the user's request wait for the slow work.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: For Queues, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Queues first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA background worker can consume queued jobs in order without making the user's request wait for the slow work.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA growing production service may use Queues when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a background worker can consume queued jobs in order without making the user's request wait for the slow work. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Assuming delivery means exactly once without defining the actual semantics.\n- Ignoring consumer lag or poison messages.\n- Forgetting that partitioning and key choice affect ordering and throughput.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Queues, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
