    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe usual flow for Profiling is to start with the inputs or conditions, apply the behavior described here, and verify the resulting state. Profiling measures where a program spends CPU time, memory, or other resources so optimization targets observed bottlenecks.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the mechanism and can explain a sequence, not just the end result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Establish the starting condition.\n2. Apply the mechanism: Profiling measures where a program spends CPU time, memory, or other resources so optimization targets observed bottlenecks.\n3. Verify the result using the concrete case: When CPU usage rises, profile the running workload to find the hot path before changing code based on intuition alone.\n4. Watch the trade-off or failure condition: For Profiling, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nWalk through the normal sequence in order. Explain what changes at each important step, then mention how you would verify the result and what you would inspect if the operation failed.\n\n**Example :**\nWhen CPU usage rises, profile the running workload to find the hot path before changing code based on intuition alone.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA growing production service may use Profiling when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, when CPU usage rises, profile the running workload to find the hot path before changing code based on intuition alone. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Profiling as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Profiling.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the steps. That is more useful than listing commands or buzzwords.",
