    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Profiling misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Profiling. Profiling measures where a program spends CPU time, memory, or other resources so optimization targets observed bottlenecks.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Profiling: Profiling measures where a program spends CPU time, memory, or other resources so optimization targets observed bottlenecks.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: When CPU usage rises, profile the running workload to find the hot path before changing code based on intuition alone.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: For Profiling, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nWhen CPU usage rises, profile the running workload to find the hot path before changing code based on intuition alone.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA growing production service may use Profiling when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, when CPU usage rises, profile the running workload to find the hot path before changing code based on intuition alone. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Profiling as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Profiling.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
