    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nProfiling is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. For Profiling, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what Profiling gives you: Profiling measures where a program spends CPU time, memory, or other resources so optimization targets observed bottlenecks.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: For Profiling, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: When CPU usage rises, profile the running workload to find the hot path before changing code based on intuition alone.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nWhen CPU usage rises, profile the running workload to find the hot path before changing code based on intuition alone.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA growing production service may use Profiling when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, when CPU usage rises, profile the running workload to find the hot path before changing code based on intuition alone. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Profiling as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Profiling.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
