    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nQuery tuning improves database performance by examining SQL, data distribution, indexes, execution plans, and access patterns.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Query tuning accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Query tuning improves database performance by examining SQL, data distribution, indexes, execution plans, and access patterns.\n2. Use a concrete case: Compare the query plan, row counts, indexes, and actual execution time before and after a tuning change.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Faster reads can come with extra storage, write overhead, or plan complexity, so the access pattern should justify the change.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Query tuning first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nCompare the query plan, row counts, indexes, and actual execution time before and after a tuning change.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA growing production service may use Query tuning when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, compare the query plan, row counts, indexes, and actual execution time before and after a tuning change. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Relying on intuition instead of the actual query plan.\n- Ignoring row counts and data distribution.\n- Fixing one query while creating a write, memory, or locking problem elsewhere.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Query tuning, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
