    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nI would explain Query tuning in plain language first, then give the technical detail that distinguishes it from nearby concepts. The practical anchor is: Compare the query plan, row counts, indexes, and actual execution time before and after a tuning change.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can communicate the concept clearly at an interview pace and adjust the depth when the interviewer asks a follow-up.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Give a one-sentence definition: Query tuning improves database performance by examining SQL, data distribution, indexes, execution plans, and access patterns.\n2. Explain one important behavior or distinction.\n3. Use the example to make it concrete: Compare the query plan, row counts, indexes, and actual execution time before and after a tuning change.\n4. Finish with the limitation: Faster reads can come with extra storage, write overhead, or plan complexity, so the access pattern should justify the change.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would keep the first answer concise, then wait for the interviewer to signal whether they want internals. If they go deeper, I would expand the mechanism and trade-offs rather than repeating the definition.\n\n**Example :**\nCompare the query plan, row counts, indexes, and actual execution time before and after a tuning change.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA growing production service may use Query tuning when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, compare the query plan, row counts, indexes, and actual execution time before and after a tuning change. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Relying on intuition instead of the actual query plan.\n- Ignoring row counts and data distribution.\n- Fixing one query while creating a write, memory, or locking problem elsewhere.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nPractice saying {c} aloud in your own words. A technically correct answer is weaker if you cannot explain it without reading a script.",
