    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Feature flags misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Feature flags. Feature flags decouple deployment from user exposure by allowing behavior to be enabled, disabled, or targeted without redeploying the code.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Feature flags: Feature flags decouple deployment from user exposure by allowing behavior to be enabled, disabled, or targeted without redeploying the code.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: A risky feature can be deployed behind a flag, enabled for a small audience, and rolled back by configuration without reverting the entire application version.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: For Feature flags, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nA risky feature can be deployed behind a flag, enabled for a small audience, and rolled back by configuration without reverting the entire application version.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA growing production service may use Feature flags when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a risky feature can be deployed behind a flag, enabled for a small audience, and rolled back by configuration without reverting the entire application version. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Feature flags as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Feature flags.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
