    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Failure design misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Failure design. Failure design plans explicitly for dependency failures, partial outages, retries, degraded modes, and recovery rather than treating failure as an exception to the architecture.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Failure design: Failure design plans explicitly for dependency failures, partial outages, retries, degraded modes, and recovery rather than treating failure as an exception to the architecture.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: If a dependency becomes unavailable, decide whether the operation should fail, degrade, queue work, or return cached data based on the business requirement.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: For Failure design, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nIf a dependency becomes unavailable, decide whether the operation should fail, degrade, queue work, or return cached data based on the business requirement.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA growing production service may use Failure design when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, if a dependency becomes unavailable, decide whether the operation should fail, degrade, queue work, or return cached data based on the business requirement. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Failure design as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Failure design.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
