    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “What would you change about Failure design when the system grows or an important assumption stops being true?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to Failure design.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: Failure design plans explicitly for dependency failures, partial outages, retries, degraded modes, and recovery rather than treating failure as an exception to the architecture.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: If a dependency becomes unavailable, decide whether the operation should fail, degrade, queue work, or return cached data based on the business requirement.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: For Failure design, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nIf a dependency becomes unavailable, decide whether the operation should fail, degrade, queue work, or return cached data based on the business requirement.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA growing production service may use Failure design when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, if a dependency becomes unavailable, decide whether the operation should fail, degrade, queue work, or return cached data based on the business requirement. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Failure design as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Failure design.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
