    shortDescription: "Frequently asked Senior Software engineer 5+ Years of expirance interview question about Architecture decisions.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nArchitecture decisions record and justify important system-level choices, including constraints, alternatives, and trade-offs.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Architecture decisions accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Architecture decisions record and justify important system-level choices, including constraints, alternatives, and trade-offs.\n2. Use a concrete case: For a new service, record the decision, constraints, alternatives considered, and consequences instead of documenting only the chosen technology.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: The design can improve separation and independent change, but more boundaries also introduce coordination, deployment, and debugging complexity.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Architecture decisions first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nFor a new service, record the decision, constraints, alternatives considered, and consequences instead of documenting only the chosen technology.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA large production codebase may use Architecture decisions when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, for a new service, record the decision, constraints, alternatives considered, and consequences instead of documenting only the chosen technology. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Designing for scale before identifying the actual bottleneck.\n- Ignoring failure recovery and operational ownership.\n- Treating an architectural pattern as a guarantee rather than a trade-off.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Architecture decisions, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
