    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Technical debt misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Technical debt. Technical debt is the future cost created by shortcuts or deferred design work that makes later changes more expensive or risky.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Technical debt: Technical debt is the future cost created by shortcuts or deferred design work that makes later changes more expensive or risky.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: If a fragile module slows every release, the team can reserve capacity to simplify it rather than continuing to add workarounds around the same defect.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: For Technical debt, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nIf a fragile module slows every release, the team can reserve capacity to simplify it rather than continuing to add workarounds around the same defect.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA large production codebase may use Technical debt when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, if a fragile module slows every release, the team can reserve capacity to simplify it rather than continuing to add workarounds around the same defect. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Technical debt as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Technical debt.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
