    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nTechnical debt is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. For Technical debt, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what Technical debt gives you: Technical debt is the future cost created by shortcuts or deferred design work that makes later changes more expensive or risky.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: For Technical debt, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: If a fragile module slows every release, the team can reserve capacity to simplify it rather than continuing to add workarounds around the same defect.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nIf a fragile module slows every release, the team can reserve capacity to simplify it rather than continuing to add workarounds around the same defect.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA large production codebase may use Technical debt when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, if a fragile module slows every release, the team can reserve capacity to simplify it rather than continuing to add workarounds around the same defect. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Technical debt as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Technical debt.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
