    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “What would you change about Technical debt when the system grows or an important assumption stops being true?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to Technical debt.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: Technical debt is the future cost created by shortcuts or deferred design work that makes later changes more expensive or risky.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: If a fragile module slows every release, the team can reserve capacity to simplify it rather than continuing to add workarounds around the same defect.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: For Technical debt, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nIf a fragile module slows every release, the team can reserve capacity to simplify it rather than continuing to add workarounds around the same defect.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA large production codebase may use Technical debt when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, if a fragile module slows every release, the team can reserve capacity to simplify it rather than continuing to add workarounds around the same defect. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Technical debt as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Technical debt.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
