    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nIt matters because scalability is the ability to handle increased workload while staying within acceptable performance and reliability limits. In practice, the example shows how that behavior is used: a search service may scale horizontally by adding instances, but only if the data layer, connection pool, and state management can support the added load.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe question is looking for reasoning rather than another definition. Explain the problem the concept solves, then say what would make you choose a different approach.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State the problem or need.\n2. Explain how Scalability addresses it: Scalability is the ability to handle increased workload while staying within acceptable performance and reliability limits.\n3. Tie that benefit to the concrete situation: A search service may scale horizontally by adding instances, but only if the data layer, connection pool, and state management can support the added load.\n4. State the condition that can make the approach a poor fit: Improving headroom or speed usually consumes additional compute, engineering effort, or operational complexity, so the target should be tied to observed demand.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nAnswer the “why” directly, then connect it to a real engineering constraint. I would give one concrete situation and close by saying what would make me choose another approach.\n\n**Example :**\nA search service may scale horizontally by adding instances, but only if the data layer, connection pool, and state management can support the added load.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA large production codebase may use Scalability when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a search service may scale horizontally by adding instances, but only if the data layer, connection pool, and state management can support the added load. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Designing for scale before identifying the actual bottleneck.\n- Ignoring failure recovery and operational ownership.\n- Treating an architectural pattern as a guarantee rather than a trade-off.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nDo not repeat the definition as your whole answer. Explain the problem, the practical value, and the condition that would change your decision.",
