    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Scalability misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Scalability. Scalability is the ability to handle increased workload while staying within acceptable performance and reliability limits.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Scalability: Scalability is the ability to handle increased workload while staying within acceptable performance and reliability limits.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: A search service may scale horizontally by adding instances, but only if the data layer, connection pool, and state management can support the added load.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: Improving headroom or speed usually consumes additional compute, engineering effort, or operational complexity, so the target should be tied to observed demand.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nA search service may scale horizontally by adding instances, but only if the data layer, connection pool, and state management can support the added load.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA large production codebase may use Scalability when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a search service may scale horizontally by adding instances, but only if the data layer, connection pool, and state management can support the added load. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Designing for scale before identifying the actual bottleneck.\n- Ignoring failure recovery and operational ownership.\n- Treating an architectural pattern as a guarantee rather than a trade-off.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
