    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “How would you change your Scalability design when traffic, failure rate, or organizational constraints change?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to Scalability.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: Scalability is the ability to handle increased workload while staying within acceptable performance and reliability limits.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: A search service may scale horizontally by adding instances, but only if the data layer, connection pool, and state management can support the added load.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: Improving headroom or speed usually consumes additional compute, engineering effort, or operational complexity, so the target should be tied to observed demand.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nA search service may scale horizontally by adding instances, but only if the data layer, connection pool, and state management can support the added load.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA large production codebase may use Scalability when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a search service may scale horizontally by adding instances, but only if the data layer, connection pool, and state management can support the added load. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Designing for scale before identifying the actual bottleneck.\n- Ignoring failure recovery and operational ownership.\n- Treating an architectural pattern as a guarantee rather than a trade-off.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
