    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nCapacity planning estimates resources needed to support expected workload while leaving appropriate safety margin for peaks and failure scenarios.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Capacity planning accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Capacity planning estimates resources needed to support expected workload while leaving appropriate safety margin for peaks and failure scenarios.\n2. Use a concrete case: Estimate expected traffic, peak load, resource consumption, and growth before deciding whether current infrastructure can support the next workload.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Improving headroom or speed usually consumes additional compute, engineering effort, or operational complexity, so the target should be tied to observed demand.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Capacity planning first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nEstimate expected traffic, peak load, resource consumption, and growth before deciding whether current infrastructure can support the next workload.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA large production codebase may use Capacity planning when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, estimate expected traffic, peak load, resource consumption, and growth before deciding whether current infrastructure can support the next workload. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Capacity planning as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Capacity planning.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Capacity planning, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
