    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Capacity planning misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Capacity planning. Capacity planning estimates resources needed to support expected workload while leaving appropriate safety margin for peaks and failure scenarios.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Capacity planning: Capacity planning estimates resources needed to support expected workload while leaving appropriate safety margin for peaks and failure scenarios.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: Estimate expected traffic, peak load, resource consumption, and growth before deciding whether current infrastructure can support the next workload.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: Improving headroom or speed usually consumes additional compute, engineering effort, or operational complexity, so the target should be tied to observed demand.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nEstimate expected traffic, peak load, resource consumption, and growth before deciding whether current infrastructure can support the next workload.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA large production codebase may use Capacity planning when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, estimate expected traffic, peak load, resource consumption, and growth before deciding whether current infrastructure can support the next workload. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Capacity planning as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Capacity planning.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
