    shortDescription: "Frequently asked Senior Software engineer 5+ Years of expirance interview question about Capacity planning.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nCapacity planning is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. Improving headroom or speed usually consumes additional compute, engineering effort, or operational complexity, so the target should be tied to observed demand. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what Capacity planning gives you: Capacity planning estimates resources needed to support expected workload while leaving appropriate safety margin for peaks and failure scenarios.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: Improving headroom or speed usually consumes additional compute, engineering effort, or operational complexity, so the target should be tied to observed demand.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: Estimate expected traffic, peak load, resource consumption, and growth before deciding whether current infrastructure can support the next workload.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nEstimate expected traffic, peak load, resource consumption, and growth before deciding whether current infrastructure can support the next workload.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA large production codebase may use Capacity planning when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, estimate expected traffic, peak load, resource consumption, and growth before deciding whether current infrastructure can support the next workload. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Capacity planning as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Capacity planning.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
