    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe usual flow for Consistency is to start with the inputs or conditions, apply the behavior described here, and verify the resulting state. Consistency describes what values different readers can observe after writes under a system’s specified guarantees.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the mechanism and can explain a sequence, not just the end result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Establish the starting condition.\n2. Apply the mechanism: Consistency describes what values different readers can observe after writes under a system’s specified guarantees.\n3. Verify the result using the concrete case: After a write, decide whether the next read must see that write immediately or whether eventual convergence is acceptable for the use case.\n4. Watch the trade-off or failure condition: For Consistency, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nWalk through the normal sequence in order. Explain what changes at each important step, then mention how you would verify the result and what you would inspect if the operation failed.\n\n**Example :**\nAfter a write, decide whether the next read must see that write immediately or whether eventual convergence is acceptable for the use case.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA large production codebase may use Consistency when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, after a write, decide whether the next read must see that write immediately or whether eventual convergence is acceptable for the use case. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Consistency as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Consistency.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the steps. That is more useful than listing commands or buzzwords.",
