    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nConsistency is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. For Consistency, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what Consistency gives you: Consistency describes what values different readers can observe after writes under a system’s specified guarantees.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: For Consistency, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: After a write, decide whether the next read must see that write immediately or whether eventual convergence is acceptable for the use case.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nAfter a write, decide whether the next read must see that write immediately or whether eventual convergence is acceptable for the use case.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA large production codebase may use Consistency when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, after a write, decide whether the next read must see that write immediately or whether eventual convergence is acceptable for the use case. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Consistency as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Consistency.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
