    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe usual flow for Partitioning is to start with the inputs or conditions, apply the behavior described here, and verify the resulting state. Partitioning divides data or workload into smaller units so work and storage can be distributed.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the mechanism and can explain a sequence, not just the end result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Establish the starting condition.\n2. Apply the mechanism: Partitioning divides data or workload into smaller units so work and storage can be distributed.\n3. Verify the result using the concrete case: Partition data using a key that distributes traffic reasonably and matches the access pattern; a poor key can create a hot partition.\n4. Watch the trade-off or failure condition: For Partitioning, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nWalk through the normal sequence in order. Explain what changes at each important step, then mention how you would verify the result and what you would inspect if the operation failed.\n\n**Example :**\nPartition data using a key that distributes traffic reasonably and matches the access pattern; a poor key can create a hot partition.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA large production codebase may use Partitioning when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, partition data using a key that distributes traffic reasonably and matches the access pattern; a poor key can create a hot partition. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Partitioning as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Partitioning.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the steps. That is more useful than listing commands or buzzwords.",
